From 14cf4f95a92e5d5256dcf69fabef2b332af13489 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 14:41:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] cuda/hip: fold the MoE expert-slot reduction into the down
 matvec (+1.6% decode)

After 0034 the expert-down `mul_mat_id` writes [n_embd, n_expert_used] with the
routing weight already applied in its epilogue, and a fused multi-add sums the
eight slices into [n_embd]: 40 launches/token at 1.7us, moving 64 KB of reads
and 64 KB of writes per layer to produce 8 KB - when the matvec that wrote those
bytes held every one of them in a register.

The parallelism is the whole difficulty. In the launch this replaces
`blockIdx.y` IS the expert slot, so nrows_x * n_expert_used warps are in flight;
looping one warp over the eight experts would cut that 8x on a matvec that is
latency-bound (444 GB/s measured), costing more than the fold saves. Instead
`threadIdx.y` becomes the expert slot - it is free, because this shape trims to
a single k-split warp (calc_nwarps_launched) - so the same number of warps run,
the eight partials reduce through 32 bytes of LDS in slot order 0..7, and there
is one store instead of eight.

Two halves, only useful together:

1. `mmvq.cu`: `mul_mat_vec_q_expert_reduce`, gated to the single-k-split-warp
   shape it is derived from (one row per block, every k-block covered by one
   warp in a single pass, host trimming active), plus a dedicated entry point
   that reuses the shared q8_1 cache so falling through to
   `ggml_cuda_mul_mat_vec_q` is free.
2. `llama-graph.cpp`: the expert combine becomes in-place AT THE DECODE SHAPE,
   so `moe_out` lands on the matvec's own output block (expert slot 0).

Half 2 is not cosmetic, and it is the lesson of this round. The fold hoists the
store back to matvec time, while the kernel's other blocks are still reading the
routing weights and the ids - and out of place, ggml-alloc frees both at the
routing multiply and puts `moe_out` exactly there: 156/160 decode sites landed
on the weights, 4/160 on the ids, and the hazard scan correctly declined every
one. Slot 0 is disjoint from both by construction, because `experts` is
allocated while the ids are still live and the multiply reuses it in place while
`weights` is still live.

The first attempt at half 2 kept the weights and ids alive with dangling view
nodes instead. It worked, and it made the whole engine nondeterministic: server
greedy A/A went from 5/6 byte-exact to 0/6, with the CUDA fold disabled, and it
was not the 0032 epilogue pass or the 0030 state view (both toggled off, still
0/6). Do not pin tensors with consumerless view nodes on this fork.

Measured (runner box, HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0):

- toggle A/B (both halves off = pre-patch), 5 rounds: tg128 143.63-143.91 ->
  145.89-146.10, median per-round ratio 1.0157, 5/5 disjoint; pp512 neutral
  (arms overlap: 4280-4362 off vs 4271-4338 on)
- dispatches 821 -> 781/token; k_bin_bcast 41 -> 1/token; decode kernel time
  5.734 -> 5.686 ms/token
- whole-process vs stock, 5 rounds: tg128 median ratio 1.7979 (0036: 1.7825),
  pp512 1.3410
- gate ppl 3.9248/3.9259/3.9262 vs stock 3.9314 (-0.13 to -0.17%); prefill is
  structurally untouched (the fold requires ne[2] == 1, the in-place combine
  n_tokens == 1)
- decode-path ppl - llama-perplexity -ub 1 so every ubatch is one token and the
  fold actually fires - 3.9404 vs 3.9338 fold-off, i.e. +0.229% vs stock,
  deterministic across loads and inside the 0.5% band
- test-backend-ops MUL_MAT_ID / ADD / MUL / VIEW OK
- server greedy: A/A deterministic (5/6 byte-exact, p5 is the near-tie prompt
  that also drifts A/A in the control); vs control 2/6 byte-exact, 4/6 drift

Not byte-exact, and measured rather than assumed: GGML_CUDA_MOE_REDUCE_PER_SLOT=1
keeps the new launch geometry but stores per slot and leaves the add chain in
the graph, and it reproduces the folded result exactly. So the reduction is
exact in slot order - it is the rewritten matvec body that the compiler
contracts differently, the same class as 0024/0026/0028/0031/0033 and gated the
same way.

GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_MOE_EXPERT_REDUCE=1 disables the fold,
GGML_MOE_COMBINE_INPLACE=0 the graph half, GGML_CUDA_MOE_REDUCE_DEBUG=1 prints
one accept/decline line per site with the gate that declined.
